{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788273611,"nonce":"8rp/ApBtZbO5U4Zhna4gNzMh+nA=","message":"IMPLEMENTED on branch `spec/site-routine` as THREE commits, session 15, worker.\nBuilds clean (`dotnet build -c Release`, 0/0). NOT bench-verified — a worker\nlaunches nothing.\n\n- `7e23e7f` — (a) `SiteGate`, the shared routine. `Source/AutoRimmer/Siting.cs`.\n- `90e578e` — (b) `site-survey`. `Source/AutoRimmer/SiteVerbs.cs`.\n- `923800b` — (c) `find-rect {def}`. `Source/AutoRimmer/SpatialVerbs.cs`.\n\nAlso on the branch, ahead of these and consumed by them: `edfee42` (`8b4839f`)\nand `33e9d95`, which lands the `rot` argument and `Footprint.TryCentreFor` — the\ncorner-to-centre inverse this issue's point 2 requires. The inverse is our code\nbecause the game has none, and it is VERIFIED against `GenAdj.OccupiedRect` on\nevery call rather than trusted: it returns false if the round trip does not\nclose, because a wrong inverse is invisible until a module grid makes the\none-cell slide cumulative (`bac4eba`).\n\n\n`GenConstruct.CanPlaceBlueprintAt(def, pos, rot, map, godMode:false,\nstuffDef:stuff)` plus `Designator_Build.Visible`, published as TWO blocks —\n`verdict` and `selectable` — so \"would be refused\" and \"cannot even be selected\"\nnever read alike. Research goes through `WorldSafe.Finished`. `GateId =\n\"site-gate/1\"`, bumpable on any change to what the gates test, the same\ndiscipline `CropRenderer.AlphabetId` documents. `PlaceWorkers`' lazy init is\nrecorded and accepted; every third-party PlaceWorker call is individually\nwrapped.\n\n**`Designator_Build.Visible` has TEN clauses.** `1adc737` #1 named two and its\nverification comment #4 corrected that to six, titling the correction \"amendment\n`buildingPrerequisites` (via `ListerBuildings.ColonistsHaveBuilding`),\n`discoveryPrerequisites` (via `HiddenItemsManager.Hidden`) and\n`requireInspectedGravEngine` (Odyssey). Same class of gap #4 was arguing about: a\nverb reproducing only the research clause is a god-hand for prerequisite- and\nDLC-restricted defs. All ten ship, each as a token an agent branches on\n(`min-tech`, `research`, `building-prerequisite`, …) with the prose in `detail`.\nCommented separately on `1adc737`.\n\nTwo rulings that came out of writing it, both in DESIGN:\n\n- **The godMode clause is published and NOT honoured.** Vanilla opens `if\n  (DebugSettings.godMode) return true;`. Reproducing it would turn every player\n  verb into a god-hand the moment a dev session left the flag on, with `ok:true`\n  to show for it. Clauses are evaluated regardless; `god_mode_on` rides in the\n  envelope as a fact about the session, and a caller wanting the bypass asks a\n  `dev:*` verb, which says so itself.\n- **An unreadable research route REFUSES.** `WorldSafe.Finished` reports\n  everything unfinished if its field ref failed, so the clause\n  `research-unreadable` distinguishes \"not researched\" from \"we could not look\" —\n  `PawnSafe.Policies`'s `source` discipline applied to a gate.\n\n\n`site-survey {def, pos|at, rot?, stuff?, margin?, from?}`. Three tiers, one row\nshape (`{at, role, ok, reason, blocker}`) across all three, `Blockers.cs`'s\nblocker shape, plus the crop. Four resolutions, all in DESIGN:\n\n1. **`pos` and `at` are mutually exclusive; both is `bad-args`.** Different\n   conventions, different cells for an even-sized def; accepting both and\n   preferring one reproduces the very bench failure the verb exists to prevent.\n   `pos_source` rides in the envelope.\n2. **The overlay is a SECOND grid, not extra glyphs in the crop.** Same\n   origin/w/h, its own id `site-survey/overlay-1`, `distinct_from`\n   `map-view/ascii-1`. Mixing tier markers into the map grid would change what a\n   char means, which by `CropRenderer`'s own rule obliges a bump of\n   `map-view/ascii-1` AND of `map-dump`'s `distinct_from` — the identity\n   `accept/e6faa51-channel-alphabet.py` enforces.\n3. **The margin tier lists NOTABLE cells and tallies the rest** (fogged,\n   unstandable, roofed, doors; cap 40 with `more`; counts and the reach answer in\n   `margin_facts`). A 3x survey of an 11x6 def is 594 margin cells and 594 rows\n   of \"fine\" is not a mid-loop read. The picture carries the whole ring.\n4. **The gate tiers test fog and the identical-thing scan PER CELL** where\n   vanilla tests only the centre. Strictly more informative and it cannot\n   contradict the verdict, which is the game's own answer published beside the\n   rows rather than derived from them. Said out loud in the code.\n\n`rot` omitted is `def.defaultPlacingRot` as the Scope says. Note the deliberate\ndivergence from `dev:spawn-thing`, whose default stays `Rot4.North`: that verb\nmodels `DebugThingPlaceHelper.DebugSpawn`, which reaches the `GenSpawn.Spawn(def,\nc, map, wipeMode)` overload that hard-codes North, and **76 vanilla defs set\n`defaultPlacingRot` to something else**, so unifying would silently re-face every\nbuilding every shipped suite stages. Two verbs, two models, each citing its own.\n\nInteraction tier implements BOTH rules and keeps them apart:\n`InteractionCellStandable`'s predicate per cell, and\n`PlaceWorker_PreventInteractionSpotOverlap`'s 3x3 scan — a different rule, asked\nonce of the placement and attributed to the cell its own walk names. A chair\npasses: `DiningChair` bottoms out in `BuildingBase`, which sets no `passability`,\nand `Standable` is the enum's first value.\n\n\nEvery candidate gated by `SiteGate` — the identical routine, not a copy. `def`\nbranches at the TOP of the verb into its own routine, so the size path is\nuntouched: **the diff is 340 insertions and zero deletions**, which is what\n\"`find-rect {w,h}` output is byte-for-byte what it was\" looks like as evidence\nrather than as a claim. Six resolutions, in DESIGN:\n\n1. **One candidate per cell** — the first rotation in\n   `def.defaultPlacingRot`-first order that the gate accepts, not the cross\n   product. Four rotations of one cell are one site as far as \"where can this go\"\n   is concerned, and the cross product spends `max` four times on the same\n   ground; `rot_order` is published, and a caller wanting a facing pins `rot`.\n2. **`center` is DROPPED in def mode**, not relabelled. `CellRect.CenterCell` is\n   not the placement centre of an even-sized rect, and a field that looks like\n   the value to pass and is off by one exactly where it matters is this issue's\n   bench failure. `pos` is the argument, `at`/`w`/`h` the identity.\n3. **`dist` is measured to `pos`, the same key the ring walk terminates on.** 2.6\n   blocker 1 was terminating on one key and sorting by another, which selects the\n   wrong set rather than merely ordering it badly. Rotation rank is the tie-break\n   only.\n4. **`require` narrows and never replaces.** `buildable`/`walkable` are REFUSED\n   as subsumed — refusing beats accepting-and-ignoring, `7382bdd`'s whole\n   failure mode — `roofed`/`unroofed` are honoured, and **`reachable-from:P` now\n   tests the INTERACTION cells** (falling back to touching the footprint for a\n   def that has none). That is this issue's point-3 complaint fixed rather than\n   deferred; the echoed `require` block says which question was answered.\n5. **`Designator_Build.Visible` is asked ONCE, before the walk**, because it is\n   def-level. An unselectable def has no candidates anywhere, and a `selectable`\n   block saying which clause is a different fact from \"no room\" — and it saves\n   thousands of `CanPlaceBlueprintAt` calls, which is why the def path's examine\n   cap is 2000 against the size path's 6000 and why `gate_calls` is published.\n6. **No per-cell blocker tally in def mode.** The refusing cell of a rejected\n   candidate is frequently not the cell walked — `8b4839f` is exactly that\n   mistake once — so describing the walked cell for thousands of rejects is that\n   defect at scale. `rejected` tallies the game's own sentences, `refusals`\n   carries a few worked examples, and one `site-survey` on a chosen candidate\n   names cells for real.\n\nFog is checked over the WHOLE rect before the gate, where the game checks only\nthe centre: this file's standing rule is that the player-facing surface hides\nundiscovered cells, and it short-circuits most of the expensive calls in\nunexplored ground.\n\n\nEvery Acceptance bullet on this issue is a bench assertion and none has been run.\nThe round-trip bullet (\"each candidate's `pos` round-trips: spawning at it with\nthat `rot` yields an `OccupiedRect()` equal to the candidate's `at`/`w`/`h`, for\nall four rotations of an even-sized def\") is now cheap to assert by FIELD rather\nthan by re-derivation: `Dev.Describe` publishes `rot` for a spawned thing and\n`rect` for a multi-cell one (commit `33e9d95`).","files":null}]}